<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antanas Smetona</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antanas Smetona was the first President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, from 1919 to 1920, and returned to power after the military coup of December 1926. He ruled as an authoritarian leader of the Nationalist Union until the Soviet ultimatum of June 1940, when he fled abroad. He died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antanas-smetona.jpg" alt="Antanas Smetona" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Owned by the National Museum of Lithuania · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antanas_Smetona_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Antanas Smetona was born on 10 August 1874 in the village of Užulėnis in the Kovno Governorate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, the eighth of nine children of a farming family of former serfs. His father died when he was eleven. He attended the Palanga Progymnasium, where Lithuanian books banned by the Tsarist authorities were easier to obtain, and the Jelgava Gymnasium, and in 1897 entered the law faculty of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>. He joined Lithuanian student societies, rejected the socialist ideas he met, and was briefly imprisoned and deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> for taking part in the student protests of February 1899. He graduated in 1902.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Smetona moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, worked at the Vilnius Land Bank until 1915 and devoted much of his time to the Lithuanian press, editing papers and founding the newspaper Viltis in 1907. In September 1917 he was elected chairman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-lithuania">Council of Lithuania</a>, and on 16 February 1918 he signed the Act of Independence. On 4 April 1919 the State Council elected him the first president of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and on 19 April 1920 the Constituent Assembly elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleksandras-stulginskis">Aleksandras Stulginskis</a> in his place.</p><p>Smetona led the coup of December 1926 that deposed President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazys-grinius">Kazys Grinius</a>, and he became president again on 19 December. He dissolved parliament a year later and on 15 May 1928 promulgated a constitution with wider presidential powers. In 1929 he removed his prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustinas-voldemaras">Augustinas Voldemaras</a> and took dictatorial powers. He was re-elected in 1931 and 1938 as the only candidate, and a third constitution of 1938 kept the authoritarian character of the second. His regime repeatedly arrested members of the banned Communist Party. It also put Nazis in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Memel</a> region on trial in 1934–1935, and Memel was surrendered to Hitler in 1939. According to the account followed here, no anti-Jewish law was passed under his rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the Soviet ultimatum of June 1940 Smetona proposed armed resistance, but the government and army commanders thought it hopeless. On 15 June he handed his duties to Prime Minister Antanas Merkys and crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, then went on through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, arriving in March 1941. He settled in Cleveland, where he worked on a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and his memoirs. He died on 9 January 1944 in a fire at his son's house; the official record blamed an overheated furnace, and a Soviet cause is considered doubtful. Lithuania has held since 1990 that Merkys's takeover of the presidency was illegal, since Smetona never formally resigned. Historians still debate his legacy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antanas Smetona” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A conference in Vilnius in September 1917 elected a twenty-member Council of Lithuania, the Taryba, chaired by <b>Antanas Smetona</b>, and on 16 February 1918 its members, among them Jonas Basanavičius, signed the Act of Independence, proclaiming an independent democratic state with Vilnius as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In May 1926 the bloc led by the Lithuanian Christian Democratic Party lost its majority, and in December a military coup, supported by the Lithuanian Nationalists Union and the Christian Democrats, installed <b>Antanas Smetona</b> as president and Voldemaras as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Užulėnis, in what is now <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Died in Cleveland, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.42,24.58&amp;year=1919" aria-label="Užulėnis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Antanas Smetona"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4737 5381h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4737 5381h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.42,24.58&amp;year=1919">What was here in 1919? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
